# node-linker=hoisted puts a real copy of every package in the root node_modules
# instead of only linking into node_modules/.pnpm. It is here for one reason: pnpm
# cannot create POSIX symlinks on Windows without elevated privileges, so it uses
# NTFS junctions, and Docker Desktop does not translate a junction into the Linux
# VM. A linked package is then missing inside the container even after a successful
# `pnpm install` - and ./:/workspace is how every service gets its code.
#
# It does not remove the app-local link: apps/ai-gateway/node_modules/@aws-sdk/... is
# a symlink either way. The fix works by fall-through - the link is dead inside the
# container, Node keeps walking up, and /workspace/node_modules holds a real
# directory. Verified by pointing the app-local link at a non-existent path.
#
# What it still buys after ai-gateway started loading its dependency lazily is
# nodemon, which start.bat reaches through WATCH_POLL=1 and scripts/dev.sh. Exactly
# how that fails without this line is unverified on Windows: dev.sh guards on
# `[ ! -x node_modules/.bin/nodemon ]`, and whether that guard fires decides between
# losing live reload and the service exiting outright, because pnpm writes cmd-shim
# files rather than symlinks into .bin there. Worth settling on a Windows box, since
# it also decides whether that guard tests the right thing.
#
# The cost is pnpm's strict dependency isolation, and it is bigger than one package:
# hoisting flattens the whole transitive closure plus the root devDependencies into
# one directory every app resolves from, so an app can import something it never
# declared and neither `pnpm lint` nor `pnpm test:imports` will say so. That breaks
# first for a participant copying the pattern into a frontend outside this repo.
#
# The lockfile is linker-independent - switching left pnpm-lock.yaml untouched - so
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` in CI is unaffected. Do not remove this as
# unnecessary just because ai-gateway no longer needs it; nodemon is still linked.
node-linker=hoisted
